Welcome to Day 7 of my Journey to become a Data Engineer


🚀 DAY 7 — B-TREE INTERNALS
🎯 Goal

Today's goal was to understand how PostgreSQL retrieves data internally. Instead of focusing on SQL syntax, I wanted to understand what happens after executing a query and how indexes actually work.

My Biggest Realization

Before today, I thought indexes simply made queries faster.

Now I understand that an index is actually a separate data structure that helps PostgreSQL locate rows without scanning the entire table.

The database still needs to retrieve the actual row from the table.

The index only tells PostgreSQL where to find it.

Understanding B-Tree

A B-Tree is the default index structure used by PostgreSQL.

Instead of searching every row sequentially, PostgreSQL navigates through different levels of the tree until it reaches the correct leaf page.

Conceptually it works like this:

Root Page
      │
      ▼
Intermediate Pages
      │
      ▼
Leaf Pages
      │
      ▼
Heap (Actual Table Row)

The index does not contain the complete row.

It stores searchable values together with pointers to the actual table rows.

Planner vs Executor

One thing I learned today is that PostgreSQL has two major stages before returning data.

Planner

The planner decides the most efficient execution plan.

Examples:

Sequential Scan
Index Scan
Bitmap Heap Scan

The planner makes this decision based on statistics.

Executor

After the planner chooses the plan, the executor performs the actual work.

It follows the execution plan and retrieves the requested rows.

Importance of Statistics

One of the biggest things I learned today is that PostgreSQL depends heavily on statistics.

If statistics become outdated, the planner may choose a poor execution plan even if indexes already exist.

Possible solution:

ANALYZE;

ANALYZE updates the planner's statistics.

It does not reorganize the physical table.

Correlation

Today I learned what correlation means inside PostgreSQL.

Correlation measures how closely the physical order of rows matches the order of an index.

High correlation means nearby index entries usually point to nearby table pages.

Low correlation means rows are scattered across many pages.

Lower correlation can increase random disk access and reduce query performance.

CLUSTER

One solution for low correlation is:

CLUSTER;

Unlike ANALYZE,

CLUSTER physically reorganizes the table based on an index.

This improves physical locality and can improve future index scans.

VACUUM

I also learned about dead tuples.

When rows are updated or deleted, PostgreSQL does not immediately remove the old versions.

These become dead tuples.

Running:

VACUUM;

helps clean these dead tuples and recover reusable space.

Production Mindset

Today I realized that performance tuning is not simply about adding indexes.

Before making changes, a Data Engineer should understand why a query became slow.

Examples:

Large Difference Between Estimated Rows and Actual Rows

Possible cause:

Outdated statistics

Possible action:

ANALYZE;
Low Correlation

Possible cause:

Rows are physically scattered.

Possible action:

CLUSTER;
Large Number of Dead Tuples

Possible cause:

Many updates or deletes.

Possible action:

VACUUM;
Buffer Cache

Another realization today was understanding that PostgreSQL does not always read directly from disk.

Frequently accessed pages are stored in shared buffers.

If the requested page is already in memory, PostgreSQL can avoid expensive disk reads.

My Production Thinking

Today I stopped thinking:

"How do I make this query faster?"

Instead, I started asking:

"Why did PostgreSQL choose this execution plan?"

Understanding the planner is much more valuable than memorizing SQL syntax.

Biggest Lesson Today

Today wasn't about learning another SQL command.

It was about understanding how PostgreSQL thinks before executing a query.

I realized that becoming a Data Engineer is not just writing SQL.

It is understanding how the database makes decisions so I can make better optimization choices.

